  "articleBody": "Hanging new drywall might seem straightforward, but a few common slip-ups can really mess up your finish. Let's talk about how to avoid them:\n\n1.  **Misaligned Seams:** This is a big one. If your sheets aren't perfectly aligned, you'll end up with uneven surfaces that are a nightmare to mud. Always double-check alignment before fastening.\n2.  **Insufficient Fastening:** Not enough screws, or screws that don't hit the studs, means loose sheets and potential cracks later. Make sure you're using the right length screws and hitting your marks.\n3.  **Not Staggering Joints:** Never line up all your horizontal or vertical joints in the same place. Staggering them, like brickwork, adds strength and reduces the chance of noticeable cracks.\n4.  **Tight Floor Contact:** Leaving your drywall directly on the floor can lead to moisture wicking and damage. Always leave a small gap—around a half-inch—at the bottom.\n5.  **Poor Handling Around Openings:** Corners, windows, and doors need careful attention. Incorrect cuts or weak fastening here can lead to cracks. Use proper techniques like 'L' cuts around openings to prevent stress cracks.\n\nAvoiding these common mistakes ensures a much smoother finish down the line. If you're tackling a big project, like a basement remodel in Aurora or a new addition in Parker, getting professional help for the hanging can save you a lot of grief.",
